  "overview": {
    "text": "In mathematics, infinity describes what has no finite bound or end; the symbol ∞ is not an ordinary counting number. The idea appears in different ways: a sequence can grow without bound, a limit can increase without bound, and a set can contain infinitely many elements. Set theory compares the sizes of sets by asking whether their elements can be paired one-to-one. This reveals that the natural numbers, integers, and rational numbers share one countable size, ℵ₀, while the real numbers form a larger, uncountable set. Georg Cantor’s work made comparing infinite collections precise and showed that infinity is not just one size.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://nrich.maths.org/articles/all-about-infinity",
      "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/infinity/",
      "https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Cantor/"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "A set is countably infinite if its elements can be matched one-to-one with the natural numbers; ℵ₀ names this cardinality.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://nrich.maths.org/articles/all-about-infinity",
        "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/infinity/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Although the integers and rational numbers may seem more numerous than the natural numbers, each can be listed in a way that pairs its elements one-to-one with them.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://nrich.maths.org/articles/all-about-infinity",
        "https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Cantor/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Cantor’s diagonal argument shows that the real numbers cannot all be placed in a one-to-one list with the natural numbers, so their cardinality is larger than ℵ₀.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://nrich.maths.org/articles/all-about-infinity",
        "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/infinity/",
        "https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Cantor/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Cantor proved the real numbers were uncountable in work published in 1874, helping establish the mathematical study of different sizes of infinity.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Cantor/",
        "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/infinity/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In modern set theory, there is no largest cardinality: for any set, the set of all its subsets has a strictly larger cardinality.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/infinity/"
      ]
    }
  ],
